<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>14: The wave equation</title>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Let's consider the wave equation in a string of length <m>L</m>
  where both ends are clamped:
  <md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} \amp  = \amp  v^2\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial
      x^2}</mrow>
<mrow>u(0,t) \amp  = \amp  0</mrow>
<mrow>u(L,t) \amp  = \amp  0</mrow>
</md>
  Find the general solution to this equation.  (What technique do you think you can apply here?)</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <introduction>
      <p>The wave equation describes the acceleration of the string so we
  need to specify its initial position and initial velocity.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>How does the form of the solution simplify when the string is
  initially at rest: <m>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}(x,0) = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How does the form of the solution simplify when the string is
  initially at equilibrium: <m>u(x,0) = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that the string is initially at rest and <m>u(x,0) =
  f(x)</m>.  The double angle formulas say that
  <md>
    \sin(\alpha)\cos(\beta) = \frac12\left(\sin(\alpha+\beta) +
      \sin(\alpha-\beta)\right).
  </md>
  Use this fact to show that the wave breaks into two pieces: a left-
  and right-travelling wave.
  <md>
  u(x,t) = \frac12(f(x+vt) + f(x-vt)).
  </md></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>How do the solutions change if the ends are ``free'' rather than
  clamped: 
  <md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(0,t) \amp  = \amp  0</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(L,t) \amp  = \amp  0</mrow>
</md></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
